  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Light rain precedes an early week cooldown.\n\nLow pressure off the east coast will gradually lift northward\nthrough the day, though the vast majority of the precipitation\nassociated with this system will remain well to our east. Even\nthough most of Central Pennsylvania will remain dry through the\nrest of the day, a few showers could still move across \nsoutheastern portions of the forecast area later this afternoon \nand evening as the low continues to move north and east. A cold\nfront tracking through Pennsylvania tomorrow morning will bring\nthe chance for a few light showers to the region, with chances\ndecreasing the farther east you get.\n\nMuch colder air moves in behind the front and temperatures \nMonday night will fall into the 20s and 30s for most locations.\nA compact shortwave moving across the Great Lakes will trigger \nscattered lake effect snow showers to develop across northern\nPA, potentially making it as far south as I-80. Lowering\ninversion heights in response to high pressure building in from\nthe west will bring a quick end to the lake effect showers\nduring the day on Tuesday, leading to a 24-36 hour period of\ndry, but cool weather. Tuesday night may be the coldest night of\nthe season so far as mainly clear skies and light winds will\nallow for temperatures to fall into the teens and 20s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Large storm system to bring rainy/breezy\nconditions for Thanksgiving.\n\nConfidence continues to increase in a strong low pressure system\nmoving up the east coast on Thursday, bringing widespread\nprecipitation to much of the northeastern United States on\nThanksgiving. The trend has been towards a more amplified upper \nlevel trough developing over the central part of the country \nTuesday and Wednesday, which has resulted in the majority of \nGEFS and EPS members showing a westward shift in the track of \nthe surface low and its associated impacts. Rain moves into \nCentral PA from the south Thursday morning and quickly spreads \nnorthward into the afternoon. Depending on how fast the \nprecipitation arrives, there may still be enough cold air in \nplace to support a mix of rain and snow, before changing to all \nrain by the afternoon. Ensemble mean QPF continues to be in the \n1 to 2 inch range for this storm.\n\nWe will also have to monitor the potential for strong wind \ngusts with this system, particularly over the southeastern third\nof the CWA. Recent runs of some of the deterministic models, \nincluding the GFS, ECMWF, AIGFS, and the AIFS have shown a 60 to\n80 knot 850 mb low-level jet developing in response to the \nstrong temperature/pressure gradient between the deepening low\nto the south and retreating high pressure to the north. It \nremains to be seen how much of this wind will be able to make it\ndown to the surface, but soundings from the GFS show 50 knot \nwinds at the top of the mixed layer Thursday afternoon over the \nLower Susquehanna Valley. The NBM 24 hour maximum wind gust \nproduct also shows a 50 percent chance of wind gusts exceeding \n45 mph over Lancaster County. Exact details regarding the \nevolution of this system will become more clear over the next \nday or two, but confidence is increasing in rain and wind \nimpacts over Central PA on Thanksgiving.\n\nColder air will gradually work its way back into Pennsylvania\nlate Thursday night into Friday as the low begins to move off to\nthe north which should allow for some snow to begin mixing in\nwith the rain, especially over the higher terrain of\nnorthwestern PA and down into the Laurel Highlands. Rain and\nsnow showers could linger through the day on Friday and\npotentially into Saturday along with continued gusty winds as a\ntight pressure gradient will remain in place over Pennsylvania.",
